  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 308 PM EDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nCurrent radar imagery indicates that this morning's precipitation \nhas cleared out of the area. Visible satellite shows mid-level cloud \ncover also moving out of the U.P. as its associated system \nprogresses eastward and is replaced with a relatively dry air mass. \nCloud cover is expected to stick around this evening in the form of \nlow-level cumulus clouds currently building in response to diurnal \nheating in the western U.P. as the cloud cover higher aloft leaves \nthe area. \n\nTonight's low temperatures are expected to be warmer than average \nthanks to the cloud cover sticking around. Light southerly winds are \nexpected across the U.P., which, combined with high RH from this \nmorning's precipitation, could lead to the development of fog \novernight. Thursday is expected to stay mostly cloudy and mainly \ndry. A prevailing zonal pattern is forecast across the area Thursday \nmorning due to a combination of a 500mb longwave trough over central \nCanada and large-scale ridging over the SE CONUS. At the surface, a \ncold front associated with a low in Ontario is expected to move \nacross the area Thursday morning, causing winds to increase and \nshift to northwesterly, daytime RH to drop, and skies to clear. \nGiven the relatively warm lake surface and northwesterly winds, \nthere is potential for a stray lake effect rain shower or two, \nhowever, any showers that reach the ground will be brief with \nminimal accumulation. Clear skies will result in below-average low \ntemperatures Thursday night, resulting in a frost potential around \n20-50% for the interior.\n\nThe cooling trend continues Friday, with seasonably cool highs in \nthe upper 50s. Breezy conditions out of the northwest are expected \nthroughout the day near the southeastern Lake Superior lakeshore, \nwith gusts around 30mph. Lake effect showers may also return to the \neastern U.P. on Friday, but will again remain spotty and light. \nFrost concerns return Friday night as lows reach the upper 20s to \nlow 30s, about 8-10 degrees below average. Winds die down Friday \nnight into Saturday as a surface high moves into the region, but are \nexpected to return along with chances for precipitation Saturday \nnight into Sunday as a weak clipper system progresses into southern \nOntario. Precipitation totals are expected to remain low (around a \n30% chance of 24-hour accumulations exceeding a tenth of an inch), \nhowever, chances for precipitation are widespread across the U.P. \nSunday also brings the next best chance for gales, although a large \namount of model uncertainty remains. The NBM's highest chances are \naround 30% off the coast of Luce County, with 15-20% in the rest of \nsoutheastern Lake Superior. The LREF is much less optimistic, with \ngale chances remaining out of the forecast entirely. Looking ahead \nto the start of next week, temperatures are expected to remain \nrelatively cool through Tuesday.",
